# LithosAI

One note for everything LithosAI-related: the provider, this checkout's client
for it, what the live API reveals about the serving stack, and the company's
research lineage. Per-model request behaviour — the roster table, the sampling
constraints, and the `strict` finding — lives in `src/remote/lithos_models.md`
and is not repeated here.

Compiled 2026-10-04 from live probing on this machine and public sources. Each
claim says whether it was observed or inferred.

## The provider

- Hosted, OpenAI-compatible inference over open-weight models: Kimi K3,
  DeepSeek V4.1 Flash, GLM-5.3, Qwen 3.8, GPT OSS, and others.
- API root `https://api.lithosai.cloud/v1`. Docs <https://docs.lithosai.com>,
  console <https://console.lithosai.cloud>.
- Auth: `Authorization: Bearer <key>`; the key comes from `LITHOSAI_API_KEY` or
  omp's credential store.
- Implemented: `GET /models`, `GET /models/{author}/{slug}`, and
  `POST /chat/completions`, streamed and not. `/completions`, `/embeddings`,
  `/responses`, `/batches` answer 404.
- Company: *gpuOS, Inc.* (the copyright holder in Motus's license). Founded by
  Dimitrios Skarlatos (CEO) and Zhihao Jia (CTO), both CMU CS professors.
  <https://www.lithosai.com/company>

## In this checkout

| Artifact | What it is |
| --- | --- |
| `src/remote/lithos.zig` | The client: `models` and `chat`, streamed and not; `ReasoningEffort`; tools; both error shapes as values. |
| `src/remote/lithos_models.zig` | Generated model roster; `zig build --build-file ./build.research.zig lithos_models` rewrites it from `GET /v1/models`. |
| `src/remote/lithos_models.md` | Per-model constraints (thinking, sampling) and the tool/`strict` findings. Source of truth for those. |
| `src/remote/keys.zig` | `Provider.lithosai` — where the key is read. |
| `research/lithos/lithos_probe.zig` | `zig build --build-file ./build.research.zig lithos_probe` — probes per-model `reasoning_effort` and `top_p`. |
| `research/lithos/lithos_strict_probe.zig` | `zig build --build-file ./build.research.zig lithos_strict` — probes whether `strict` is enforced. |
| `research/lithos/lithos_roles_probe.zig` | `zig build --build-file ./build.research.zig lithos_roles` — probes the message and role shapes the endpoint accepts, raw HTTP; the seventh role it found is written up in `src/remote/lithos_models.md`. |
| `research/lithos/lithos_sysdev_probe.zig` | `zig build --build-file ./build.research.zig lithos_sysdev` — probes `system` against `developer`, first against later, by codeword. |
| `research/lithos/lithos_media_probe.zig` | `zig build --build-file ./build.research.zig lithos_media` — probes how image, audio and video input are carried, raw HTTP plus one typed request; findings in `src/remote/lithos_models.md`. |
| `research/lithos/media/` | The probe's own `probe.png` (`ZEBRA 42`), `probe.mp4` and `probe.wav`, sent as base64 data URLs. |
| `research/lithos/latency.dj` | Time-to-first-token against the official DeepSeek API on identical prompts: cache behaviour, transport, and the scale curve. |
| `src/remote/omp-keys.py` | Reads the key from omp's `agent.db`; shared with every provider, not Lithos-specific. |

Client-side shape worth remembering: `chat.Function.strict` is a plain `?bool`
beside `parameters`, a pre-encoded JSON Schema string. There is no schema builder
here — the typed `StrictSchema` (which derives `required` and
`additionalProperties`) belongs to the DeepSeek client in `src/remote/deepseek.zig`.

## Tool calls and `strict`

Summarised from `src/remote/lithos_models.md` (live, 2026-10-04,
`deepseek-ai/DeepSeek-V4.1-Flash`):

- `strict: true` is accepted and *enforced*: it compiles `parameters` into a
  decoding constraint, so the model cannot emit arguments the schema forbids. The
  discriminator was an `enum: ["Paris"]` against a prompt asking for Tokyo —
  `strict: true` returned `{"city":"Paris"}`, `strict: false` returned
  `{"city":"Tokyo"}`, stable over four runs.
- It is *not* OpenAI's strict contract: a schema missing `required` or
  `additionalProperties: false`, or with `additionalProperties: true`, was accepted
  at 200 under `strict: true`. Nothing validates the schema server-side.
- Neither the field nor the meaning is in the vendor's OpenAPI reference.

## Image and video input

Measured 2026-10-05, written up per model in `src/remote/lithos_models.md`, with
the probe in `research/lithos/lithos_media_probe.zig`. In short:

- **Images work, by reference and inline.** `image_url` with an http(s) URL is
  fetched by the endpoint and read by the model; the same field carries a
  base64 data URL, which is the only "upload" the wire has — there is no
  `/files`. Both `deepseek-ai/DeepSeek-V4.1-Flash` and `moonshotai/Kimi-K3` read
  images.
- **Audio and video are not served.** `video_url`, `input_audio` and
  `audio_url` are part types the API layer knows and gates per model: Kimi-K3
  refuses all three (`Kimi-K3 supports image input only`), and DeepSeek accepts
  them but delivers nothing — no load error even for unreachable URLs, no media
  tokens, and the model reports the part as missing or unsupported.
  `input_video` and `file` (with `file_url` or `file_id`) are not part types at
  all. There is no upload endpoint, so audio and video have no working path in
  or out.
- **Neither shape is documented.** The reference leaves `content` as a bare
  `string|array|null`. `chat.Part` types `text` and `image_url`, which is
  everything that works.

## What the wire reveals about the engine

Observed against `api.lithosai.cloud` on 2026-10-04:

- Every `/chat/completions` response carries the header
  `x-llm-d-flow-queue-duration-ms`. That is llm-d's flow-control admission metric,
  defined as `FlowQueueDurationHeaderKey` in
  <https://github.com/llm-d/llm-d-router/blob/main/pkg/epp/metadata/consts.go>.
  So the request path runs through *llm-d*, the CNCF-sandbox Kubernetes
  inference control plane (Red Hat, Google Cloud, IBM Research, CoreWeave,
  NVIDIA) that routes *above* a model server.
- Engine errors come back in vLLM's OpenAI-server shape, character for character.
  Rejecting `top_p: 0.5` on `moonshotai/Kimi-K3`:

```json
{"object":"error","message":"top_p must be between 0.95 and 1.0 for this model; got 0.5","type":"BadRequestError","param":null,"code":400}
```

- The vendor's OpenAPI accepts `top_k` and calls it a "Non-standard sampling
  parameter" — also a vLLM extra parameter, absent from the OpenAI schema.
- No `server`, `via`, or request-id header is exposed; `/health`, `/metrics`,
  `/version`, `/docs`, `/openapi.json` all 404. Only `/v1/*` exists.

`[INFERENCE]` The model server is vLLM or a fork of it: the error envelope, the
`top_k` extension, and the grammar-constrained `strict` behaviour all point that
way, but a fork keeps the error classes and only headers and error bodies were
read. The llm-d gateway is observed, not inferred. "Lithos Engine" is the
marketing name for their closed layer on top of that stack.

## HTTP versions

Observed 2026-10-05 on `api.lithosai.cloud:443`, both through this machine's
resolver (every host here maps into the proxy range 198.18.0.0/15) and against
each of the three A records with `--resolve` to the real address from DoH, so
the proxy is out of the path. Both readings agree.

- *HTTP/2 — yes.* The handshake negotiates `h2`: `openssl s_client -alpn
  h2,http/1.1` prints `ALPN protocol: h2` on all three addresses, and `curl`
  uses HTTP/2 by default (`GET /v1/models` answers `401` with
  `http_version=2`). HTTP/1.1 is served too, and a client that offers no ALPN
  (which is what `std.http.Client` below does) gets HTTP/1.1.
- *HTTP/3 — no.* No response from the API host carries `Alt-Svc` — checked on
  the `401` of `/v1/models`, the `404` of `/`, and the `404` of the API root —
  and `curl --http3-only` to each of the three addresses times out. That is the
  endpoint, not this sandbox: the same command against `cloudflare-quic.com`'s
  real address returns HTTP/3 `200`, so UDP egress works.
- The *docs and marketing hosts differ*. `docs.lithosai.com` (Cloudflare) and
  `lithosai.com` (CloudFront) both send `alt-svc: h3=":443"; ma=86400`, and
  `docs.lithosai.com` answers HTTP/3 `200`. Only the inference API's own edge
  stops at h2.

Re-run (no key needed; an unauthenticated `401` carries the same edge headers):

```sh
curl -sS -D - -o /dev/null https://api.lithosai.cloud/v1/models
echo | openssl s_client -connect api.lithosai.cloud:443 -alpn h2,http/1.1 2>/dev/null | grep ALPN
curl -sS --http3-only -o /dev/null https://api.lithosai.cloud/v1/models
```

Consequence for this checkout: the h2 the edge offers is unreachable from
`src/remote/lithos.zig`. `std.http.Client` in Zig 0.17.0 is HTTP/1.1 only — no
`alpn`/`h2` identifier exists anywhere under `std/http`, so no ALPN extension is
sent and every request here is HTTP/1.1.

## Source code

No public source for the engine. GitHub orgs `LithosAI-Org` and `lithos-ai` show
no public repositories in my fetch. The engine is closed and also sold on-prem
("Lithos Engine, deployed on your own standard GPUs").

Their one open-source project is *Motus*, on the agent side.

## Motus

<https://github.com/lithos-ai/motus> · PyPI `lithosai-motus` · Apache-2.0 ·
"Copyright 2026 gpuOS, Inc."

An open-source *agent-serving* project plus a Python library for writing the
agents it serves. Relevant capabilities:

- `motus serve` exposes any agent as a session-based HTTP API locally; `motus
  deploy` ships it to Motus Cloud. Self-managed or cloud, "same code, any scale".
- Serves agents written in Motus, the OpenAI Agents SDK, the Anthropic SDK,
  Google ADK, or plain Python — "change the import, keep your code".
- `@agent_task` dataflow runtime: dependency graph inferred from data flow,
  automatic parallelism, declarative retries/timeouts/backoff with per-call
  `.policy()`.
- Docker-sandbox tool provider; input/output guardrails; auto-tracing with an
  HTML viewer, Jaeger export, and a cloud dashboard; human-in-the-loop
  pause/ask/resume; prompt caching via `CachePolicy`.

Versus omp (this harness): the serving/deployment surface is the real gap — omp
has no way to run its agent as a hosted, scalable HTTP endpoint, and its embedding
surfaces are in-process (Bun SDK) or stdio (`--mode rpc` / `acp`). Everywhere the
two overlap (memory, compaction, MCP, multi-provider, subagents, prompt caching,
human-in-the-loop), omp matches or exceeds. Motus is a serving framework; omp is
an interactive coding harness.

## Research lineage

The company name and the engine's framing come from the founders' systems
research.

- *LithOS* — "An Operating System for Efficient Machine Learning on GPUs",
  arXiv 2504.15465 (2025). A GPU-OS prototype: a TPC scheduler, transparent
  kernel atomization, right-sizing, and power management. Skarlatos is a
  co-author. `[INFERENCE]` The company name is taken from this paper; no code
  links the engine to it.
- *Zhihao Jia's serving/systems work* (all confirmed from the paper records):
  - *SpecInfer* — tree-based speculative inference and verification; small
    models draft a token tree, the LLM verifies it in parallel. 1.5–2.8× over
    distributed serving, 2.6–3.5× over offloading. arXiv 2305.09781.
  - *FlexFlow Serve* — low-latency serving via tree-based speculative
    inference; the serving side of FlexFlow.
  - *FlexFlow Train* — auto-discovers parallelization strategies for
    distributed DNN training (Jia, Zaharia, Aiken). The repo is now split into
    `flexflow-train` and `flexflow-serve`.
  - *SpotServe* — LLM serving on preemptible/spot instances: dynamic
    reparallelization, migration as bipartite matching (Kuhn–Munkres), stateful
    inference recovery. 2.4–9.1× lower P99, 54% cheaper. arXiv 2311.15566.
  - *Mirage* — multi-level superoptimizer for tensor programs via μGraphs
    (kernel / thread block / thread levels), up to 3.3×. arXiv 2405.05751.
- Correction to an earlier claim: *FlexGen is not Jia's.* Its authors are the
  Stanford/HazyResearch group (Sheng, Zheng, Yuan, Li, Ryabinin, Fu, Xie, Chen,
  Barrett, Gonzalez, Liang, Ré, Stoica, Zhang). Jia's line is FlexFlow /
  SpecInfer / SpotServe / Mirage.

